The service validates in a fixed order (type, availability, member limit), then mutates both objects and returns a `Loan` record. Using `isinstance(book, Borrowable)` means only classes that declare the ABC can be lent. Only `library.py` is the solution; `models.py` is the supplied starter.

**`library.py`**

```python
from models import Borrowable, Loan


class Library:
    def lend_book(self, member, book):
        if not isinstance(book, Borrowable):
            raise TypeError("Item is not borrowable")

        if not book.is_available:
            raise ValueError("Book not available")

        if not member.can_borrow():
            raise ValueError("Borrow limit reached")

        book.is_available = False
        member.increment_borrow()

        return Loan(member, book)

    def return_book(self, member, book):
        book.is_available = True
        member.decrement_borrow()
```

**`models.py`**

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod


class User:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name


class Member(User):
    def __init__(self, name, borrow_limit=3):
        super().__init__(name)
        self.borrow_limit = borrow_limit
        self.borrowed = 0

    def can_borrow(self):
        return self.borrowed < self.borrow_limit

    def increment_borrow(self):
        self.borrowed += 1

    def decrement_borrow(self):
        if self.borrowed > 0:
            self.borrowed -= 1


class Borrowable(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def borrow(self):
        pass


class Book(Borrowable):
    def __init__(self, title):
        self.title = title
        self.is_available = True

    def borrow(self):
        return self.title


class Novel(Book):
    pass


class Loan:
    def __init__(self, member, book):
        self.member = member
        self.book = book
```

Note for review: `return_book` does not verify that the member actually holds the book. A hardened version should raise a domain error. Run the tests with `pytest -q`.

